module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FormalGeometry.DerivedCompletionRinged.FiniteTypeIdealPending099E
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FormalGeometry.DerivedCompletionRinged.KoszulPerfectDual
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FormalGeometry.DerivedCompletionRinged.PushforwardCommutesCompletionGenerated
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FormalGeometry.DerivedCompletionRinged.PushforwardCompletionUniversalProperty

/-!
# `Rf_*` and derived completion at a globally generated ideal sheaf, via the Koszul towers

[Stacks, Tag 0A0G] asserts that `Rf_*(K^∧) = (Rf_*K)^∧` for an **arbitrary** `K ∈ D(𝒪')`, where
`ℐ ⊂ 𝒪` is a finite type sheaf of ideals and `ℐ' ⊂ 𝒪'` is the ideal generated by
`f^♯(f^{-1}ℐ)`. The source proves it by reducing to an `ℐ` generated by global sections
`f_1, …, f_r`, rewriting both sides by the Koszul formula of [Stacks, Tag 0A0E], exchanging
`Rf_*` with `R lim` ([Stacks, Tag 0A07]) and finishing with the projection formula
[Stacks, Tag 0944] together with the identification
`Lf^*K(𝒪, f_1^n, …, f_r^n) = K(𝒪', g_1^n, …, g_r^n)` of the pulled-back Koszul complexes.

`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.nonempty_iso_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_of_isDerivedLimit`
of `FiniteTypeIdealPending099E.lean` runs that argument at an arbitrary finite-type pair of ideal
sheaves, taking three inputs: [Stacks, Tag 099F] as two instance binders, [Stacks, Tag 0A0E] as
derived-limit presentations `hsource` and `htarget` of the two completions, and
[Stacks, Tag 0944] as an isomorphism `hproj` of the two towers. When the two sheaves of ideals
are generated by finitely many global sections, which is the regime the source's own proof
reduces to, the first two are supplied here:

* [Stacks, Tag 099F] comes from the generators by
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_generatedIdealSheaf`, as it
  does throughout `PushforwardCommutesCompletionGenerated.lean`;
* [Stacks, Tag 0A0E] is
  `RingedSite.isDerivedLimit_tensorDerivedDualSystem_koszulPowerSystem_completion`
  at the Koszul towers on `g_1, …, g_r` and on `t_1, …, t_s`. That theorem presents the *Čech*
  completion `R𝑆heafHom_𝒪(Č(f), -)` of [Stacks, Tag 099B] as the derived limit, and the reflector
  of [Stacks, Tag 099F] is a different functor with the same universal property, so the two have
  to be identified first. That identification is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.reflectorToCompletionFunctor` below.

The remaining input is `hproj`, i.e. [Stacks, Tag 0944].
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionHomTower.lean` avoids it by running the same argument in the
Koszul Hom tower.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.reflectorToCompletionFunctor`: the canonical map from the derived
  completion of [Stacks, Tag 099F] to the Čech completion of [Stacks, Tag 099B], at an ideal
  sheaf generated by finitely many global sections.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletionUnit_comp_reflectorToCompletionFunctor`: that
  map is compatible with the two completion maps out of `K`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_reflectorToCompletionFunctor`: it is invertible — the
  reflector of [Stacks, Tag 099F] at a globally generated ideal sheaf **is** the Čech completion
  of [Stacks, Tag 099B], objectwise and compatibly with the units.
* `RingedSite.nonempty_iso_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_generatedIdealSheaf_of_natIso`:
  `Rf_*(K^∧) ≅ (Rf_*K)^∧` for an **arbitrary** `K`, at two sheaves of ideals generated by
  finitely many global sections, with [Stacks, Tag 0944] as the only input.

## Implementation notes

*Why the two generating families are unrelated.* The source takes `ℐ' ⊂ 𝒪'` to be the ideal
generated by `f^♯(f^{-1}ℐ)`, so that generators of `ℐ` determine generators of `ℐ'`. For a
*general* morphism of sites there is no naive transport of a global section of `𝒪` to one of
`𝒪'`: a global section in the sense of `CategoryTheory.Presheaf.globalSections` is a compatible
family over every object of the site, while `f^♯` produces sections only over the objects in the
image of `f.u`.

That obstruction lifts as soon as the target site has a terminal object whose image is terminal —
in particular at ringed spaces and at schemes, where `f.u = Opens.map f.base` and `Opens.map_top`
is `rfl`. The transport is `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.pushGlobalSections` of
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionConstructedIdeal.lean`, and
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.eq_generatedIdealSheaf_pushGlobalSections` there proves the
resulting ideal sheaf **is** the source's `f^{-1}ℐ · 𝒪_X` — least among those receiving
`f^♯(f^{-1}ℐ)` — so `PushforwardIdealCompatible` is discharged from the data rather than
assumed.

In this file the two generating families remain independent, and the
source's relation between the two ideals is the hypothesis
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.PushforwardIdealCompatible`, exactly as in
`PushforwardCommutesCompletionGenerated.lean`. That hypothesis is recorded in the last statement
and not consumed by its proof: the derived-limit argument does not use it, and the conclusion is
an isomorphism of objects rather than invertibility of the canonical comparison map, whose
construction does use it.

*Objects, not the canonical map.* The conclusion is the display as the source writes it,
`Rf_*(K^∧) = (Rf_*K)^∧`, and not the stronger statement that
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison` is invertible, which is
what the source's proof actually establishes. Uniqueness of derived limits is non-canonical, so
the isomorphism produced here is not known to be that map. It cannot be upgraded through
`isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_isIso_map_unit` either: by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_map_derivedCompletionUnit_iff` of
`PushforwardCompletionUniversalProperty.lean` that criterion's hypothesis holds only when `Rf_*K`
is already derived complete, so it is strictly stronger than the conclusion.

*The reflector against the Čech completion.* The map
`reflectorToCompletionFunctor` is the adjunct, under the reflector adjunction of
[Stacks, Tag 099F], of the Čech completion map `K ⟶ R𝑆heafHom_𝒪(Č(g), K)`; so it is canonical,
and its compatibility with the two units is `Equiv.apply_symm_apply` for the hom-set equivalence.
Its invertibility is the universal property of the Čech completion,
`bijective_precomp_completionUnit_generatedIdealSheaf` of `GeneratedIdealAdjoint.lean`, in the one
direction and `bijective_derivedCompletionUnit_comp` of
`PushforwardCompletionUniversalProperty.lean` in the other: each produces the inverse against
which the other's injectivity identifies the two composites with identities.

*The countable products.* Every `Limits.HasProduct` the derived limits need is produced inside the
proofs by `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.hasProductsOfShape_nat_derivedCategory_ringSheaf`, which
comes from K-injective resolutions and so needs no exactness of countable products. The
`Limits.HasCoproduct` arguments cannot be produced that way — countable coproducts of `D(𝒪)` would
need the countable products of `Mod(𝒪)` to be exact — and are instance arguments, as they are on
[Stacks, Tag 0A0E] itself.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0944](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0944)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 099B](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/099B)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 099F](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/099F)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A07](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A07)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A0E](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A0E)
